🛠 Environment Overrides
Docker Compose supports multiple files:

Base file (docker-compose.yml) → defines common services.

Override file (docker-compose.override.yml) → applied automatically for dev.

Production file (docker-compose.prod.yml) → applied explicitly.


🚀 Usage

# Development (default: uses docker-compose.yml + docker-compose.override.yml)
docker-compose up --build -d

# Production (explicitly specify prod file)
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up --build -d


🔗 Key Differences


Environment	Backend Command	DB Name	Logging
Development	python app.py --reload	appdb_dev	Debug
Production	gunicorn with 4 workers	appdb	Error


📉 Benefits
Clean separation of dev vs prod configs.

No duplication — base file holds shared definitions.

Flexibility — easy to add staging/test overrides later.


